WEBVTT

NOTE Approximate sentence/phrase timings within each matching narration segment.

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.107 line:76% position:50% size:90%
This is a short tour of ValueLens in Power
BI, using demo data.

2
00:00:04.107 --> 00:00:08.323 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Start with Activation: how many licensed
people are actually active?

3
00:00:08.323 --> 00:00:12.974 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The headline cards give the overall
picture, and the department chart shows

4
00:00:12.974 --> 00:00:14.587 line:76% position:50% size:90%
where enablement may help.

5
00:00:14.587 --> 00:00:18.803 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Check the date range and organisation
filter before comparing teams.

6
00:00:18.803 --> 00:00:22.400 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The figures shown here are examples, not
customer results.

7
00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:24.811 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Next, open Adoption.

8
00:00:24.811 --> 00:00:30.051 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The trend separates licensed, unlicensed,
agent and Cowork sessions per user.

9
00:00:30.051 --> 00:00:34.271 line:76% position:50% size:90%
This helps distinguish a sustained habit
from a one-off spike.

10
00:00:34.271 --> 00:00:39.171 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Use the audience buttons and date slicer
to keep comparisons consistent.

11
00:00:39.171 --> 00:00:44.819 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The activity mix adds context about what
people do; a high session count alone does

12
00:00:44.819 --> 00:00:47.950 line:76% position:50% size:90%
not demonstrate better work or answer
quality.

13
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:53.281 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Task Breakdown moves from how much
activity to what kind of activity.

14
00:00:53.281 --> 00:00:58.239 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The chart groups work from asking and
finding through producing and delegating.

15
00:00:58.239 --> 00:01:01.942 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The activity breakdown and model mix
provide another angle.

16
00:01:01.942 --> 00:01:06.712 line:76% position:50% size:90%
These are rule-based categories, not the
same classification used by Copilot

17
00:01:06.712 --> 00:01:07.340 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Analytics.

18
00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:12.047 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Read them as directional evidence, rather
than a judgement about individual

19
00:01:12.047 --> 00:01:12.800 line:76% position:50% size:90%
performance.

20
00:01:13.867 --> 00:01:18.688 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Estimated Value translates observed work
into expert-equivalent hours and a

21
00:01:18.688 --> 00:01:19.652 line:76% position:50% size:90%
modelled value.

22
00:01:19.652 --> 00:01:23.638 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Check the effort scenario, hourly salary
and reporting period.

23
00:01:23.638 --> 00:01:27.367 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The bar chart shows which task categories
contribute most.

24
00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:32.445 line:76% position:50% size:90%
These are assumption-driven estimates, not
measured time saved or realised cash

25
00:01:32.445 --> 00:01:32.960 line:76% position:50% size:90%
savings.

26
00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:37.974 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Annualised value is a projection; it
should not be read as a financial outcome

27
00:01:37.974 --> 00:01:39.067 line:76% position:50% size:90%
already achieved.

28
00:01:40.117 --> 00:01:44.947 line:76% position:50% size:90%
License Readiness highlights observed
unlicensed use and ranks potential

29
00:01:44.947 --> 00:01:45.685 line:76% position:50% size:90%
candidates.

30
00:01:45.685 --> 00:01:50.850 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Compare frequency and active days, then
look at dormant licensed seats before

31
00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:51.521 line:76% position:50% size:90%
expanding.

32
00:01:51.521 --> 00:01:57.089 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A blank score means no observed unlicensed
activity, not necessarily low readiness.

33
00:01:57.089 --> 00:02:01.986 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Use the ranking to start a conversation
about needs and access, not as an

34
00:02:01.986 --> 00:02:04.267 line:76% position:50% size:90%
automatic licence-allocation rule.

35
00:02:05.283 --> 00:02:08.284 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Finally, open the Signal to Impact
appendix.

36
00:02:08.284 --> 00:02:13.673 line:76% position:50% size:90%
It exposes the human-time assumptions
behind the value estimates, with category

37
00:02:13.673 --> 00:02:15.446 line:76% position:50% size:90%
controls for your context.

38
00:02:15.446 --> 00:02:18.583 line:76% position:50% size:90%
The glossary helps decode unfamiliar
measures.

39
00:02:18.583 --> 00:02:23.835 line:76% position:50% size:90%
A simple reading order is activation,
adoption, tasks, value, then readiness.

40
00:02:23.835 --> 00:02:29.087 line:76% position:50% size:90%
Keep the date and audience consistent,
challenge the assumptions, and use the

41
00:02:29.087 --> 00:02:31.883 line:76% position:50% size:90%
report to choose one practical follow-up.
